    ]
  },
  "long_biography": {
    "plain_text": "Amazon tourism entrepreneur Roberto Rotondo sought formal protection for forest owned by his company near Nauta. As chairman of Jungle Xports, he applied for recognition of Amazon Natural Park as a private conservation area. The government granted a ten-year designation in 2011, with objectives covering native plants and wildlife, sustainable ecotourism and interpretation of the forest ecosystem.\n\nHis tourism business catered largely to overseas visitors who booked well ahead. Its small passenger fleet carried guests from Iquitos towards the Pacaya Samiria reserve, combining river travel with access to designated visitor sites. Rotondo’s daughter Mariana subsequently managed the company.",
    "html": "<p>Amazon tourism entrepreneur Roberto Rotondo sought formal protection for forest owned by his company near Nauta. As chairman of Jungle Xports, he applied for recognition of Amazon Natural Park as a private conservation area. The government granted a ten-year designation in 2011, with objectives covering native plants and wildlife, sustainable ecotourism and interpretation of the forest ecosystem.</p>\r\n<p>His tourism business catered largely to overseas visitors who booked well ahead. Its small passenger fleet carried guests from Iquitos towards the Pacaya Samiria reserve, combining river travel with access to designated visitor sites. Rotondo’s daughter Mariana subsequently managed the company.</p>\r\n",
    "word_count": 98,
